               SENATE COMMITTEE ON EDUCATION
                     Dede Alpert, Chair
           1999-2000 First Extraordinary Session
                              

BILL NO:       AB1x
AUTHOR:        Villaraigosa and Strom-Martin
AMENDED:       March 4, 1999
FISCAL COMM.:  Yes                      HEARING DATE: March  
10, 1999
URGENCY:       No             CONSULTANT: Diane Kirkham



 <u> SUMMARY </u> 

This bill establishes the California Peer Assistance and  
Review Program for Teachers.

 <u> BACKGROUND </u> 

 <u> Mentor Program
 </u> Current law establishes the California Mentor Teacher  
Program and provides that the primary function of mentor  
teachers is to provide assistance and guidance to new  
teachers. Mentor teachers may also provide staff  
development for teachers and develop special curriculum.   
The program provides funding at the rate of $5680 per  
mentor teacher, with districts allowed to designate up to  
5% of their teachers as mentors.  Statewide, the program  
currently funds approximately 14,000 mentor teachers.

 <u> Beginning Teacher Support and Assistance (BTSA) Program
</u> Current law establishes the Beginning Teacher Support and  
Assistance Program (BTSA) to give guidance to first and  
second year credentialed teachers.  BTSA provides $3000 per  
beginning teacher with a local matching requirement for an  
additional $2000.  Many districts with BTSA programs use a  
portion of their Mentor program funds to meet the matching  
share requirement in BTSA.  The Governor's Budget proposes  
doubling the size of BTSA in 1999-2000 to serve all 24,000  
first and second year teachers in the state.

 <u> Teacher Intern and Pre-Intern Programs
 </u> Current law establishes teacher intern and pre-intern  
programs wherein individuals earn their full teaching  




                                                  AB 1X
                                                  PAGE 2



credential while employed as a teacher under an emergency  
credential.

 <u> Instructional Time and Staff Development Reform ("Buyout")  
Program
 </u> Current law provides funding for districts for three days  
of staff development that are outside of the regular  
instructional year.  This funding was established in  
exchange for the elimination of the authority of districts  
to have up to eight days of staff development in lieu of  
instructional time.

 <u> Teacher Evaluation 
 </u> Current law requires the governing board of each school  
district to evaluate certificated employee performance as  
it reasonably relates to the progress of pupils toward the  
standards of expected pupil achievement established by the  
governing board.  These "Stull Act" evaluations must be  
completed at least once each year for probationary teachers  
and once every two years for permanent teachers.

 <u> ANALYSIS </u> 
 <u> 
This bill </u> establishes the California Peer Assistance and  
Review Program for Teachers for the purpose of assisting  
veteran teachers in need of development in subject matter  
knowledge or teaching strategies. Specifically, the bill: 

1)   Eliminates the California Mentor Teacher Program on  
     July 1, 2000 and establishes in its place the  
     California Peer Assistance and Review Program, which  
     would become fully operational on the same date.

2)   Specifies that a school district that accepts state  
     funds for this program agrees to negotiate the  
     development and implementation of the program with the  
     exclusive representative of the certificated employees  
     in the school district, if there is an exclusive  
     representative of the certificated employees.

3)   Specifies that, beginning July 1, 2000, a school  
     district that does <u> not </u> participate in this program is  
     not eligible for funding for the following programs:


     a)       The Peer Assistance and Review Program ;




                                                  AB 1X
                                                  PAGE 3




     b)       The Beginning Teacher Support and Assessment  
         (BTSA) Program; 

     c)       The Instructional Time and Staff Development  
         Reform Program; and 

     d)       School development plans.  

4)   Specifies that a district that receives funding for  
     participating in the Peer Assistance and Review  
     Program may also use the funding for the BTSA program,  
     the pre-intern program, the Mentor program activities  
     as allowed on January 1, 1999 or programs that support  
     training and development of new teachers.

5)   Requires, at a minimum, the following principles to be  
     included in locally developed peer review programs:


     a)       Teacher participants in the program shall  
         have permanent status and volunteer to participate  
         or be referred for participation in the program as  
         a result of their biennial evaluation;

     b)       Performance goals for an individual teacher  
         shall be aligned with pupil learning goals;

     c)       Assistance and review shall include multiple  
         observations of a teacher during periods of  
         classroom instruction;

     d)       Sufficient staff development activities shall  
         be provided to assist teachers to improve their  
         teaching skills and knowledge;

     e)       A monitoring component with a written record  
         shall be included; 

     f)       The final evaluation of a teacher's  
         participation in the program shall be made  
         available for placement in the teacher's personnel  
         file.

6)   Requires a consulting teacher participating in this  
     program to:




                                                  AB 1X
                                                  PAGE 4




     a)       Be a credentialed classroom teacher with  
         permanent status;

     b)       Have substantial recent experience in  
         classroom

     c)       Instruction; and

     d)       Have demonstrated exemplary teaching ability.


7)   Requires a joint teacher administrator peer review  
     panel to be established by participating districts to  
     govern the Peer Assistance and Review Program.  A  
     majority of the panel shall be teachers.  The panel  
     must select consulting teachers for the program,  
     review peer review reports, and make recommendations  
     to the school district governing board regarding  
     program participants.

8)   Requires the joint peer review panel to annually  
     evaluate the impact of the Peer Assistance and Review  
     Program.  

9)   Requires the Superintendent of Public Instruction  
     (SPI), subject to the availability of funding in the  
     annual Budget Act, to contract with an independent  
     evaluator on or before December 15, 2002 to prepare a  
     comprehensive evaluation of the implementation,  
     impact, cost, and benefit of the Peer Assistance and  
     Review program.  This evaluation would be required to  
     be delivered to the Legislature, the Governor, and  
     interested parties on or before January 1, 2004.

10)  Requires that a certificated employee who receives an  
     unsatisfactory rating in the biennial evaluation to  
     participate in the Peer Assistance and Review (PAR)  
     Program, if the employing district participates in the  
     PAR program.

11)  Requires school district governing boards to evaluate  
     certificated employee performance on the progress of  
     pupils toward the state-adopted academic content  
     standards as measured by state-adopted criterion  
     referenced assessments.  




                                                  AB 1X
                                                  PAGE 5




12)  Appropriates $100 million (Proposition 98) from the  
     General Fund for the 1999-2000 fiscal year, with $16.8  
     million for Planning for the new Peer Assistance and  
     Review Program and $83.2 million for the existing  
     Mentor Teacher Program. Requires the Superintendent of  
     Public Instruction to apportion to participating  
     school districts in the 1999-2000 fiscal year $1,000  
     for each mentor teacher funded by the state in  
     1999-2000. 

13)  States the intent of the Legislature that the funding  
     for 2000-2001 and beyond for the Peer Assistance and  
     Review Program shall be at least $100 million, with  
     each district's share to be the same as it received in  
     1999-2000 for the Mentor Program plus the planning  
     funds for Peer Assistance and Review. 

 <u> STAFF COMMENTS
 </u> 
 <u> 1)   Penalty for Failure to Implement </u> 
The bill provides that a district that fails to implement a  
     program of peer assistance and review by July 1, 2000  
     shall receive no funds for the following programs:

a)        Beginning Teacher Support and Assessment (BTSA)  
     ($72 m)
b)        Instructional Time & Staff Development Reform  
     ("Buyout") ($222 m)
c)        School development plans ($20 m)
d)        Peer assistance and review ($100 m).

Most major educational organizations have argued that this  
     penalty is too severe, and  that the penalty  
     inappropriately jeopardizes reforms that should be  
     strengthened, not threatened.

 <u> Staff Recommendation:
 </u> Staff recommends that the bill be amended to provide that  
     <u> only </u> the Peer Assistance and Review funds ($100  
     million) would be contingent on implementation of this  
     new program.

 <u> 2)   Timeline </u> 
The bill requires that a district negotiate an agreement  
     and implement the new program by July 1, 2000.  Many  




                                                  AB 1X
                                                  PAGE 6



     experienced with the complexities of collective  
     bargaining have argued that this is a very ambitious  
     timeline.  However, if the severe penalty for  
     nonimplementation is removed as recommended in #1  
     above, the required implementation date of July 1,  
     2000 is perhaps more tolerable.   <u> If, </u> however, the  
     severe penalty for nonimplementation is retained in  
     the bill, then a later date (perhaps July 1, 2001)  
     would be more appropriate.

 <u> 3)   Some districts underfunded?
 </u> Many districts currently use Mentor Program funds to meet  
     the match requirement in the BTSA program (assistance  
     for 1st and 2nd year credentialed teachers) or to  
     operate teacher Intern programs (training and support  
     for teachers not yet fully credentialed).  Therefore,  
     districts with higher percentages of beginning  
     teachers may have few or no Mentor funds left (after  
     meeting required BTSA match) to utilize for  
     implementation of a peer review program.



 <u> Staff Recommendation:
 </u> Staff recommends that the bill be amended to specify that a  
     district is required to implement a Peer Assistance  
     and Review Program only to the extent that it has  
     funds to do so after meeting its required matching  
     obligations in BTSA and intern teacher programs.

 <u> 4)   Future Funding Formula Needed </u> 

The bill provides for calculation of initial apportionments  
     for the Peer Review program but is not clear on the  
     funding formula to be used after the first year.

 <u> Staff Recommendation: </u> 
The bill should be amended to provide for an ongoing fund  
     distribution formula.

 <u> 5)   Technical Amendment Needed </u> 

Staff understands that the author intends to allow funds  
     for the Peer Assistance and Review Program to be also  
     expended for the purposes of the intern teacher  
     program.  Due to an oversight, the amendment has not  




                                                  AB 1X
                                                  PAGE 7



     been incorporated in the bill as of yet.

 <u> Staff Recommendation
 </u> The bill should be amended to allow Peer Assistance and  
     Review funds to be expended for the purposes of  
     teacher intern programs. 


 <u> SUPPORT </u> 

None received on this version of the bill.

 <u> OPPOSITION </u> 

None received on this version of the bill.<u> 
 </u> 
































